from authoring import finish,ref,step,paragraph
finish('metric-tsp-subtour-four-thirds',
 id='TCS-6589',title='The 4/3 conjecture for the metric TSP subtour relaxation',area='Approximation algorithms and hardness of approximation',criterion='tightness',question_type='yes_no',
 formal='Let Γ=sup_{n≥3,c} OPT_TSP(c)/OPT_LP(c), where c ranges over all metrics on n labeled points and OPT_LP is the subtour relaxation defined below. Is Γ=4/3?',
 definitions='For V={1,…,n} and E={{u,v}:u≠v}, a metric has c_uu=0, c_uv=c_vu>0 for u≠v, and c_uv≤c_uw+c_wv. Costs are arbitrary real numbers; no Euclidean embedding is assumed. OPT_TSP is the minimum sum of edge costs on a Hamiltonian cycle of the complete undirected graph. For S⊆V, δ(S) contains edges with exactly one endpoint in S, and x(F)=Σ_{e∈F}x_e. Define OPT_LP=min Σ_e c_e x_e subject to x(δ({v}))=2 for every v, x(δ(S))≥2 for every nonempty proper S⊊V, and 0≤x_e≤1 for every e. The LP is feasible and has positive optimum. The supremum ranges over every finite size, not one fixed n. This is the symmetric metric subtour-LP integrality gap, not an approximation ratio measured only against OPT_TSP, an asymmetric TSP relaxation, or a restricted geometric metric.',
 answer_criterion='Known constructions establish Γ≥4/3. Thus an affirmative resolution proves OPT_TSP(c)≤(4/3)OPT_LP(c) for every finite metric instance. A negative resolution gives an instance with ratio strictly greater than 4/3, or proves such an instance exists. A supremum need not be attained to make the question meaningful. Improving an algorithm’s upper bound while it remains above 4/3 is progress, not a resolution.',
 context=[
 paragraph('The relaxation allows fractional edge purchases while retaining two elementary properties of every tour: each vertex has degree two, and every nontrivial cut is crossed at least twice. It therefore knows much more than a disconnected cycle-cover relaxation, but its feasible points need not be convex combinations of Hamiltonian cycles. The conjecture asks for the exact worst loss caused by that remaining freedom.'),
 paragraph('For example, a feasible fractional vector may put positive weight on several alternative connections at one vertex without choosing a single predecessor and successor. The cut inequalities ensure aggregate connectivity, yet they do not force these local choices to assemble into one integral cycle. The proposed factor 4/3 says that, in a metric, repairing all such inconsistencies never costs more than one third of the fractional optimum.'),
 paragraph('The metric assumption is what makes shortcutting possible. If a connected Eulerian multigraph visits a vertex several times, replacing a detour by the direct edge between its endpoints does not increase cost. One can consequently search for a suitably cheap Eulerian structure rather than construct a Hamiltonian cycle at every intermediate step. This observation explains why spanning trees and parity correction recur in approximation algorithms for the problem.'),
 paragraph('A minimum spanning tree followed by a minimum-cost matching on its odd-degree vertices yields the classical 3/2 guarantee. Wolsey’s analysis measures both components against the LP: the tree costs at most the fractional optimum, and the matching at most half that value. The inherited July 2026 paper recalls this argument before studying a particular randomized refinement.','primary'),
 paragraph('An algorithmic ratio and an integrality-gap ratio must use the correct denominator. A guarantee cost≤α·OPT_TSP does not establish OPT_TSP≤α·OPT_LP, since the fractional optimum can be smaller. Karlin–Klein–Oveis Gharan’s FOCS 2022 result explicitly supplies an LP-relative guarantee below 3/2, extending the earlier approximation breakthrough. It is this later theorem that directly improves the general gap bound.','kko'),
 paragraph('The modern approach samples a spanning tree from a distribution constrained by fractional edge marginals, then corrects its parity. Tree cost and parity-repair cost interact, so treating the two separately can lose the desired saving. Gurvits–Klein–Leake strengthen probability estimates using capacities of real stable polynomials. Their Lemma 4.5 is explicitly LP-relative and yields the certified bound Γ≤3/2−2.18·10^{−34}. The small constant reflects a worst-case proof, not a claim that practical tours are that close to 3/2.','gkl'),
 paragraph('A class can have a better integrality gap than the worst ratio of a particular algorithm on it. Jin–Klein–Williamson prove the 4/3 bound for half-integral cycle-cut instances using a specialized construction. Half-integral means that nonzero fractional coordinates are 1/2 or 1, with an additional restriction on the hierarchy of tight cuts. This result does not cover every half-integral instance, still less every subtour solution.','cycle43'),
 paragraph('Their July 2026 paper, which supplied the inherited reference, proves a 10/7 guarantee for the maximum-entropy algorithm on that same restricted class. Since 10/7 exceeds 4/3, it improves understanding of the chosen algorithm rather than improving the already-known class gap. The authors also identify examples giving a lower bound of 11/8 on the algorithm’s performance. Such an algorithm-specific obstruction cannot refute the conjecture about the cheapest possible tour.','primary'),
 paragraph('Two complementary approaches restrict the fractional solution in other ways. Villa–Vercesi–Barta–Mastrolilli prove the 4/3 bound when an optimal solution has at most n+6 nonzero components, combining structural reasoning and computation. This is a support-size condition, not a theorem for every instance on at most n+6 vertices. Larger support remains outside its stated guarantee.','support'),
 paragraph('Cook–Hougardy–Petrich’s September 8, 2026 revision reports exact verification for all metric instances through n=16 and for half-integral extreme points through n=18. It also repairs omissions in earlier extreme-point lists. This is stronger evidence than sampling finitely many random metrics, because the computation organizes the relevant polyhedral cases. It still leaves unbounded n, and the catalogue has not rerun that enumeration.','enumeration'),
 paragraph('A September 2 preprint by Zhao Song claims a larger universal saving, ε★>2.05522·10^{−30}, through a refined maximum-entropy analysis. Its main theorem is explicitly relative to OPT_LP. The statement was checked, but the lengthy proof and numerical certificate were not independently verified here, so the value is recorded as a recent claim rather than silently replacing the established bound. Even if confirmed, it would remain far above the requested ratio 4/3.','song')],
 why='The exact strength of the principal relaxation for a canonical optimization problem, joining polyhedral structure, probabilistic rounding and approximation. A resolution would explain a longstanding gap between fractional connectivity and integral tours.',
 importance=dict(score=95,method='editorial',assessed_on='2026-09-11',reason='A defining integrality-gap conjecture in combinatorial optimization with consequences for LP rounding and the limits of metric TSP approximation methods.'),
 progress=[step('1980','Wolsey’s LP-relative analysis gives the classical upper bound 3/2, as recalled in the inherited reference.','primary'),step('2022','Karlin–Klein–Oveis Gharan prove the general LP integrality gap is at most 3/2−ε for an absolute ε>10^{−36}.','kko'),step('2024','Gurvits–Klein–Leake improve the explicit LP-relative saving to 2.18·10^{−34}; Lemma 4.5 supplies the relevant denominator.','gkl'),step('2025','Jin–Klein–Williamson’s journal article proves 4/3 for the half-integral cycle-cut class; its preprint first appeared in 2022.','cycle43'),step('2025-11-12','Villa–Vercesi–Barta–Mastrolilli revise the theorem for optimal LP solutions with at most n+6 nonzero coordinates.','support'),step('2026-07-03','The inherited revised paper gives a 10/7 guarantee for the maximum-entropy algorithm on a class whose gap is already at most 4/3.','primary'),step('2026-09-02','Song claims an LP-relative saving exceeding 2.05522·10^{−30}; recorded as a fresh unverified preprint claim, not a resolution.','song'),step('2026-09-08','Cook–Hougardy–Petrich extend their reported exact verification to n=16 generally and n=18 for half-integral extreme points.','enumeration')],
 references=[ref('primary','Maximum Entropy is a 10/7-Approximation Algorithm for the TSP on Half-Integral Cycle Cut Instances','Billy Jin; Nathan Klein; David P. Williamson',2026,'https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.01536v2','Inherited reference, first posted 1 July and revised 3 July 2026; §1 states the LP, classical bound, and distinction between class gap and algorithm ratio'),ref('kko','A (Slightly) Improved Bound on the Integrality Gap of the Subtour LP for TSP','Anna R. Karlin; Nathan Klein; Shayan Oveis Gharan',2022,'https://arxiv.org/abs/2105.10043v3','FOCS 2022; revision 11 April 2022; explicit expected tour cost relative to the subtour LP'),ref('gkl','From Trees to Polynomials and Back Again: New Capacity Bounds with Applications to TSP','Leonid Gurvits; Nathan Klein; Jonathan Leake',2024,'https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.09072v2','ICALP 2024; revision 9 May 2024; §4.2, Lemma 4.5 and Corollary 4.6 yield the LP-relative 2.18·10^{−34} saving'),ref('cycle43','A 4/3-Approximation Algorithm for Half-Integral Cycle Cut Instances of the TSP','Billy Jin; Nathan Klein; David P. Williamson',2025,'https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.04639v2','Mathematical Programming B 210, 511–518 (2025); preprint November 2022, revision July 2023; all critical tight sets must be cycle cuts'),ref('support','The Integrality Gap of the Traveling Salesman Problem is 4/3 if the LP Solution Has at Most n+6 Non-zero Components','Tullio Villa; Eleonora Vercesi; Janos Barta; Monaldo Mastrolilli',2025,'https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.07003v2','First posted 9 July, revised 12 November 2025; support-size restriction on an optimal LP solution'),ref('enumeration','Extending Exact Integrality Gap Computations for the Metric TSP','William Cook; Stefan Hougardy; Moritz Petrich',2026,'https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.12995v5','First posted 13 March, revised 8 September 2026; §§1–3, all instances through n=16 and half-integral extreme points through n=18; computational verification not rerun locally'),ref('song','A Sharper Explicit Bound on the Subtour-LP Integrality Gap for Metric TSP','Zhao Song',2026,'https://www.preprints.org/manuscript/202609.0140/v1','Posted 2 September 2026; Theorems 1–2 and Lemma 26 claim ε★>2.05522·10^{−30}; proof and certificate not independently audited here')],
 status='source_open',status_note='The general 4/3 equality remains open through the primary-source review of 11 September 2026. The established explicit upper bound recorded here is 3/2−2.18·10^{−34}; a September 2 preprint claims a further small improvement, clearly separated from verified literature. Restricted-class theorems and finite-size verification do not resolve the universal conjecture.',formulation_reviewed_on='2026-09-11')
